# Day 18-19 Summary: AWS Setup and First Terraform Deployment

Date: 16 Oct 2026

## Goal
Prepare a safe AWS account and Terraform tooling, then prove that Terraform can create and destroy a real EC2 instance. This is the base for the portal's provisioning feature.

## Day 18: AWS account and tools

### Account
- AWS free plan with about $119.99 credits, valid until 31 Jan 2027. A free-plan account stops working when credits run out and is not billed unless the owner upgrades to a paid plan.
- MFA enabled on the root account (virtual authenticator app).
- Project region: ap-south-1 (Mumbai).

### Least-privilege user for Terraform
- IAM user `terraform-portal` with no console password.
- Policy: AmazonEC2FullAccess only.
- CLI access key created and stored outside the project folder.
- Security incident handled: the first secret key appeared in a screenshot, so it was deactivated, deleted and replaced. `aws configure` was run again with the new key.

### Tools installed (via winget)
- AWS CLI 2.38.1
- Terraform 1.16.5
- Check: `aws sts get-caller-identity` returned the account and `user/terraform-portal` (not root).

## Day 19: First Terraform configuration

Folder: `terraform/`

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| main.tf | AWS provider, latest Amazon Linux 2023 image lookup, default VPC and subnet lookup, security group, EC2 instance |
| variables.tf | request_id, instance_type, storage_gb, allowed_cidr, region, with validation |
| outputs.tf | instance_id, public_ip, instance_state |
| terraform.tfvars | values for one request (kept out of git) |
| .gitignore | excludes state files, .terraform and tfvars |

### Safety rules in the code
- instance_type is limited to t2.micro and t3.micro.
- storage_gb is limited to 8-30.
- Resources are tagged Project=ec2-portal and RequestId=<id>.

### Commands and results
| Command | Result |
|---|---|
| terraform init | AWS provider v5.100.0 installed |
| terraform validate | Success, configuration valid |
| terraform plan | 2 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy (instance and security group) |
| terraform apply | Created in about 15 seconds: instance i-05af7fbde6a33e84c, state running, public IP 13.235.103.165 |
| terraform destroy | Resources removed to stop charges |

The instance was also verified in the AWS console (EC2, Mumbai, name portal-request-1).

## Problems met and fixes
| Problem | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| aws and terraform "not recognized" | PATH changes apply only to new shells | Closed and reopened PowerShell |
| Console-access dialog opened by mistake | Wrong button on the Security credentials tab | Cancelled; used the Access keys section |
| Secret key shown in a screenshot | Terminal echoed the pasted secret | Rotated the key and reconfigured the CLI |
| copy terraform.tfvars.example failed | Downloaded files never reached the folder | Created all files with PowerShell here-strings |

## Design decisions
- The backend never calls the AWS SDK directly; it only runs Terraform.
- Default VPC is used for now; a custom VPC per request is an optional extra.
- No key pair yet, so the instance cannot be logged into over SSH; this is intentional for the test.
- Instances are destroyed after each test session to protect credits.

